# Mobile modal presentation lifecycle

A React Native `Modal` with `presentationStyle="fullScreen"` is a real presented iOS view
controller, not a styled `View`. UIKit owns its presentation and dismissal and tolerates only one
transition at a time. Two habits that read as harmless JSX break it, and both fail the same way: an
empty black window sits over the app, with no redbox and no crash, and every `testID` beneath it is
unreachable for the rest of the session.

The symptom is deceptive. A full-screen modal hides everything behind it from accessibility, so a
wedged one that renders nothing leaves screen readers and Maestro seeing an empty screen. The app is
running fine underneath.

## Close through `visible`, never by unmounting

Keep the `Modal` mounted and let `visible` drive it. A condition wrapped *around* the element
removes it mid-presentation, so UIKit never runs the dismissal and the window it created stays up.

```tsx
// BAD — the element disappears while iOS still has it presented
{status === 'anonymous' ? (
  <Modal presentationStyle="fullScreen" visible={showAuthSheet}>…</Modal>
) : null}

// GOOD — one prop drives the transition, and `visible` already encodes the condition
<Modal presentationStyle="fullScreen" visible={showAuthSheet}>…</Modal>
```

This bites hardest when a single state change does both jobs, because then the condition that hides
the modal is the same one that unmounts it — the dismissal is skipped exactly when it was needed.

Children normally stay mounted; an unpresented modal's subtree is cheap. If a subtree is genuinely
expensive, gate the children rather than the `Modal`, and keep them rendered through the dismissal
animation or the content blanks mid-slide.

## Never dismiss one full-screen modal and present another in the same commit

UIKit cannot dismiss and present at once. The second presentation wedges, and neither modal paints.

This is rarely two deliberate modals. It is usually one modal closing while an unrelated provider,
reacting to the same state change, decides it now has something to show. Sequence them: let the
first finish dismissing, and present the second on a later state change that genuinely warrants it.

Sign-in is the recurring trigger, because `status` turns `authenticated` before the account snapshot
lands and providers reading account data can fire during that window — see
[mobile-anonymous-vs-account-features](/.cursor/rules/mobile-anonymous-vs-account-features.mdc)
§ `authenticated` arrives before the account does.

## Do

- Drive every full-screen modal from a single `visible` expression.
- Give one owner responsibility for what is presented, so two providers cannot both open a modal on
  the same state change.
- When adding a provider-level modal, ask what else reacts to the state that opens it.

## Don't

- Do not wrap a `Modal` in a conditional that can remove it while it is presented.
- Do not open a modal from a state that another modal is closing on.
- Do not treat a black screen with "element not visible" as a launch, Metro, or device failure until
  the hierarchy has been checked (**mobile-e2e-screenshots** § Maestro flow authoring gotchas).

